{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "noise": "L"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 121, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Restoring the direct input-to-output residual path where none existed gives much larger gains than swapping skip in for an existing op (exps 7, 11, 12 vs 8-10, 13)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Deleting the skip residual is far more damaging than deleting a conv on edge 3, because the skip is what makes deep stacked cells trainable (exps 38, 39 vs 41-43)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 1 already absent, removing edge 0 disconnects nodes 1 and 2 entirely, leaving only edge 3, so the cell collapses (exps 18, 19)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "When edge 0 is none, node 1 receives no input, so a conv on edge 4 has nothing to process and adds no capacity (exp 64 vs 61, 63)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "In conv-heavy cells, replacing the skip residual with another conv removes the shortcut and hurts, while in skip/pool cells it adds needed capacity (exps 72, 76 vs 73, 74)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 4 is absent, edge 5 may be the last learned path into the output, so removing it is catastrophic (exp 99 vs 98)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping a parametric conv for parameter-free pooling on the main output edge costs more than swapping out skip or none."}, {"edit_edge": 2, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With node 1 